## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. Change is determined by `===` (reference equality), not deep equality.

Primitives (`string`, `number`, `boolean`) compare by value — `"hello" === "hello"` is `true`. Objects and functions compare by identity — a new object literal `{}` is never `===` to another `{}`, even if they look identical.

This means every render of a parent creates **new references** for inline objects and functions:

```jsx
// Bad — new object reference on every parent render
<Child style={{ color: "red" }} />

// Bad — new function reference on every parent render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething(id)} />
```

Even if `Child` is wrapped in `React.memo`, these props always appear "changed," so `Child` re-renders every time the parent does.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component, skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
- Use when: a child renders often but its props rarely change.
- Useless if you pass new object/function references on every render (see above).

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value, recomputes only when deps change.
- Use when: a computation is expensive, or you need a stable object reference to pass as a prop.
- Not for everything — the memoization itself has overhead.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function, returns the same reference when deps are unchanged.
- Use when: you're passing a callback to a `React.memo` child or a hook dependency list.
- Equivalent to `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.

Rule of thumb: reach for these **after** you've identified a real performance problem, not preemptively.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer of a context re-renders when the context **value** changes. The classic mistake:

```jsx
// Bad — new object literal created on every render of App
function App() {
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      <Tree />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Every render of `App` creates a new `{ user, setUser }` object, so every consumer re-renders — even if `user` and `setUser` didn't change.

**Fix:** memoize the context value, and split contexts if consumers need different slices of state.

```jsx
function App() {
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user, setUser]);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={value}>
      <Tree />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [items, setItems] = useState(initialItems);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleDelete = (id) => setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id));

  // New array reference every render (even if items/query unchanged)
  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query));

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onDelete={handleDelete} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ items, onDelete }) => { /* ... */ };
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` or `query` actually change:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [items, setItems] = useState(initialItems);

  // Stable reference — only changes when setItems changes (never)
  const handleDelete = useCallback(
    (id) => setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id)),
    [] // setItems is stable, so deps are empty
  );

  // Stable reference — only recomputes when items or query change
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query)),
    [items, query]
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onDelete={handleDelete} />
    </>
  );
}

// Skips re-render if filtered and handleDelete haven't changed
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ items, onDelete }) => { /* ... */ });
```

The `React.memo` + `useCallback` + `useMemo` trio only works when applied together — any one missing breaks the optimization.
